# std/bash-tool's image (design/caos-expr.md, Phase 3): build this directory as
# a Rust project with rustc — the same shape as rgrep. `--src:@=.` hands rustc
# this tree (Cargo.toml + src/; rustc skips the caos metadata — .caos-expr,
# DEPS, DEEP-DEPS/), named by a LOCAL deep-deps mount (declared in DEPS) rather
# than /std, so std carries no ambient self-reference. No runner is named: rustc
# DEPENDS on the pool and curries the built binary onto it itself, so the result
# is curry(runner, worker1=<the compiled binary>) — a ready-to-run bounded bash
# tool in the shared runner pool.
#
# THE HELP IS BOUND HERE, so this tool describes itself. It was a hand-written
# `json!` declaration inside llm-step, which is also where `@writer` had to be
# known by matching the name "bash" -- and a copied description drifts (the
# same note std/merge/.caos-expr carries, for the same reason).
#
# `{array}` on `paths` is why this could not move sooner: every `@param` was a
# string, and declaring `paths` as one would have silently narrowed the tool
# from a list to a single value.
#
# WHAT THE HELP IS FOR. A model reads this once, before its first call, and the
# commonest mistake is leaving a path out of `paths`. That mistake does not say
# so: the scan in src/main.rs only turns "Permission denied" into a retry hint,
# while the same missing path makes `cd` say "Not a directory", makes `ls` show
# a link into /cas, and makes `grep -r` and `find` print nothing at all (they do
# not follow links). So the help names every one of those symptoms next to the
# rule, and shows one complete call, rather than leaving the model to infer the
# rule from a sentence about laziness. Keep the examples in step with
# std/README.md ("Using bash-tool") and the client repo's AGENTS.md.
HELP=<<END
Run sh -c over a scratch copy of the CONVERSATION TREE: your files, imports/ and feature/. It is not this machine's disk, and nothing in it is a git checkout.

THE ONE RULE: content is lazy. Put in `paths` every existing file or directory the command reads, edits, copies or moves. A directory brings everything under it, source trees included. Anything not listed is a placeholder, and the command does not always say so. These all mean "add that path to `paths` and run it again": Permission denied; Not a directory or No such file or directory about something you know exists; a link into /cas in ls -l; and no output at all from grep -r, find or ls -R, which skip placeholders. A cd that fails leaves the rest of the command running from the root.

Example (search an import):
  run_tool(path="caos-std/bash-tool", arguments={"cmd": "grep -rn needle imports/repo/base", "paths": ["imports/repo/base"]})
Example (copy an import to edit it; mkdir the parent first, and use cp -a so the copy keeps its commit identity):
  arguments={"cmd": "mkdir -p feature && cp -a imports/repo/base feature/01-change", "paths": ["imports/repo/base"]}

New files and new directories need no `paths`. Whatever the command writes is saved to the conversation; there is no commit step, and editing files in a source tree creates its child commits. Prefer the write, edit and read tools for single files: they need no `paths`.

Not available here: git (use the log, show and diff tools), caos-std/ (it exists only in the evaluated tree: use eval_path, then read or ls with root), and the harness's own shell. The root .caos metadata is read-only; a source tree's .caos/conflicts ledger is editable.
@param cmd The command to run with sh -c.
@param {string} [cwd] Optional conversation-relative working directory; defaults to the conversation root. It must itself be in `paths`, or be a directory the command creates.
@param {array} [paths] Conversation-relative files or directories to materialize, independent of cwd. A directory includes its descendants, including source trees.
@writer
@in
END
IMAGE=run --base:@=DEEP-DEPS/rustc --src:@=.
curry --base=$IMAGE --help=$HELP
